---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const equivCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Meaning, not words', note: 'Translating words produces nonsense ("it rains cats"); translating meaning produces language. The unit of work is the message.' },
    { math: '2 · Analysis comes first', note: 'Professional translation begins by reading the source for function: what is this text doing, for whom, in what register?' },
    { math: '3 · Equivalence is a choice', note: 'Formal equivalence stays close to the source\'s shape; dynamic equivalence reproduces its effect. Neither is "the answer" — the text decides.' },
    { math: '4 · Loss is inevitable; manage it', note: 'Puns, rhythm, cultural allusion rarely survive intact. Skilled translators choose what to preserve and compensate elsewhere.' },
    { math: '5 · The reader is the test', note: 'A translation succeeds when its audience receives the effect the source audience received — measured, in certification exams, against error typologies.' },
  ],
};

const trUnitCompare = {
  "prompt": "The same sentence, two philosophies of translating it — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "word-for-word",
    "text": "pets falling from the sky — nonsense to a German or Japanese reader; accurate about the words, false about the message"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "message-for-message",
    "text": "'it is raining very hard', expressed the way a native speaker naturally would — the idea, re-coded"
  },
  "why": "The unit of work is the message, not the word."
};

const trTheorMatch = {
  "prompt": "Match each theorist to their position:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "Cicero (1st century BCE)",
      "right": "not word for word, but sense for sense"
    },
    {
      "left": "Jerome",
      "right": "the same position — scripture excepted, where the words themselves are mysteries"
    },
    {
      "left": "Roman Jakobson",
      "right": "languages differ in what they must convey, not what they may convey"
    },
    {
      "left": "Vermeer's skopos school",
      "right": "translate for the purpose of the target text — the brief, not the source, sets the goal"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Jakobson's line explains why full equivalence is impossible in principle and translation always remains possible: the message can be recoded."
};

const trLayerSort = {
  "prompt": "Translation fails at whichever layer it ignores — file each by its layer:",
  "buckets": [
    "what it says",
    "how it colours and tunes",
    "what it does"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "'the door is closed' — the dictionary proposition",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "'slender' versus 'skinny' — the emotional colouring",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "contract language versus text messages — the formality level",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "'can you pass the salt?' — a request, not a question about ability",
      "bucket": 2
    }
  ],
  "why": "Japanese encodes social hierarchy in verb endings and English does not — identical denotations can carry different social meanings, and the translator decides which layers matter for this text."
};

const trEquivBranch = {
  "root": "Three texts land on your desk. Set each position on the formal–dynamic spectrum.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Text one: a contract clause whose enforceable force lives in its wording.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Formal — keep the structure visible",
          "next": "n1",
          "feedback": "Same sentence shapes, same ambiguities — the choice for anything whose form is its meaning."
        },
        {
          "label": "Dynamic — reproduce the reader's experience",
          "next": "dyn1",
          "feedback": "The enforceable force lived in the structure; loosening it changed the meaning."
        }
      ]
    },
    "n1": {
      "text": "Position held. Text two: scripture studied for its words as mysteries.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Formal — even the ambiguities",
          "next": "n2",
          "feedback": "Form carries ritual weight; the words themselves are the object of study."
        },
        {
          "label": "Dynamic — smooth it for modern readers",
          "next": "dyn2",
          "feedback": "A study text wants the source's shape; smoothing erases what is being studied."
        }
      ]
    },
    "n2": {
      "text": "Position held. Text three: 'gird up your loins', for a modern audience.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Dynamic — 'prepare yourself for action'",
          "next": "n3",
          "feedback": "Nida's own example: the original's readers received an exhortation to readiness, not a clothing instruction."
        },
        {
          "label": "Formal — 'gird up your loins'",
          "next": "dyn3",
          "feedback": "The modern reader receives a clothing instruction where the original gave an exhortation."
        }
      ]
    },
    "n3": {
      "text": "Three positions, all chosen by function — most real texts live between the poles, sentence by sentence."
    },
    "dyn1": {
      "text": "Mispositioned — in legal documents the form is the meaning."
    },
    "dyn2": {
      "text": "Mispositioned — scripture study keeps the structure visible, even the same ambiguities."
    },
    "dyn3": {
      "text": "Mispositioned — the effect the original carried was readiness, and the reader is the test."
    }
  },
  "why": "Formal equivalence keeps the source's structure visible; dynamic equivalence reproduces the reader's experience — neither pole is 'the answer'."
};

const trInvisJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "A good translation is invisible and interchangeable with the original.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Translators make interpretive choices — visible or invisible is itself a choice."
    },
    {
      "s": "Two excellent translations of the same poem can differ substantially.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "They are two readings of one text — both may reach the effect."
    },
    {
      "s": "Equivalence to the words is the professional standard.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Equivalence to the effect is the standard."
    }
  ]
};

const trAnalCloze = {
  "text": "The classic questions of source-text analysis: what is this text %%0%% (informing, persuading, regulating, entertaining)? %%1%% is it for (specialists, consumers, regulators, children)? What is its %%2%% and tone (legalese, breezy marketing, clinical neutrality)? And what is its %%3%% — where will the translation appear, and what must it accomplish there?",
  "answers": [
    [
      "doing"
    ],
    [
      "who"
    ],
    [
      "register"
    ],
    [
      "situation"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "The commissioning client's translation brief states the last two institutionally."
};

const trTaskCompare = {
  "prompt": "One morning, two jobs — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "the liability clause",
    "text": "form is meaning — 'shall not be liable' and 'arising from' carry the enforceable force; reproduce it in the target legal system's idiom"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "the slogan",
    "text": "the meaning is rhythm, imperative warmth, brand association — rebuild them with new words, possibly a new joke; the words themselves are not the product"
  },
  "why": "Only the analysis step tells you which job you are on — and in legal, technical and medical registers a wrong term is a factual error."
};

const trSpecOrder = {
  "prompt": "Two millennia of contest resolved as a spectrum — order it, most literal first:",
  "items": [
    "word-for-word (gloss)",
    "literal",
    "faithful",
    "semantic",
    "communicative",
    "free",
    "adaptation"
  ],
  "why": "Positions are chosen per text and per sentence — the translator's skill is knowing which position a given sentence demands, and being able to defend the choice."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/translation-studies/foundational/what-translation-is">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Translation Studies, and it assumes no prior theory and no
    second language in particular — only your everyday experience of how language carries
    meaning, tone and intent. You need nothing from earlier lessons; the argument is built
    step by step, from a single failed idiom to the professional standards that test it.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why the message, not the word, is the translator's unit of work;</li>
    <li>distinguish formal from dynamic equivalence and say which text types call for each;</li>
    <li>run a source-text analysis — function, audience, register, situation — before drafting;</li>
    <li>place a sentence on the literal-versus-free spectrum and defend the placement;</li>
    <li>recognise unmanaged loss and describe how professionals compensate for it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Translation studies begins by exploding a common assumption: translation is not word
    substitution between two dictionaries. Try it on an idiom and watch it fail — a
    word-for-word rendering of "it's raining cats and dogs" tells a German or Japanese reader
    that pets are falling from the sky, which is nonsense, not translation. What the English
    sentence <em>means</em> — it is raining very hard — is an idea, and the translator's job
    is to express that idea in the target language the way a native speaker would naturally
    express it. The unit of work is the message, not the word.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="two theories of the job — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={trUnitCompare} />
  <p>
    That reframing makes translation something entirely different from what the word suggests.
    It is an act of <strong>analysis</strong> (what is this text actually doing?), of
    <strong>judgement</strong> (of all the ways to say this in the target language, which one
    reproduces the source's effect?), and of <strong>writing</strong> — because a translation
    that reads like a translation has failed at its craft, however accurate its dictionary.
    This is why translation has its own theory (two thousand years of it, starting with
    Cicero and Jerome arguing about literal versus free), its own techniques, and its own
    professional standards and exams.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="two thousand years of theory — pair each voice with its claim" widget="match" config={trTheorMatch} />
  <p>
    The foundational unit covers three connected ideas: what <em>equivalence</em> means and
    why it is a spectrum of choices rather than a single right answer; the
    <strong>source-text analysis</strong> every professional translation begins with; and the
    literal-versus-free spectrum that organises the field's oldest debate. Then two
    professional layers — managed loss and quality standards — show how the theory becomes
    a working practice.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="meaning, analysis, equivalence, managed loss" widget="equationStep" config={equivCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the five panels and watch the professional
    sequence assemble itself — meaning before words, analysis before drafting, a deliberate
    choice of equivalence, loss that is managed rather than ignored, and the reader as the
    final test. Notice that no panel says "find the right words": every panel describes a
    decision to make, which is why translation is a craft of judgement rather than lookup.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> translation is not word substitution — the message is the unit
    of work, and the craft combines analysis, judgement and writing. Next we examine the
    field's central concept, equivalence, and discover that it comes in kinds.
  </p>

  <h2>Equivalence and meaning</h2>
  <p>
    "Meaning" itself has layers, and translation fails at whichever layer it ignores. At the
    bottom sits <strong>denotation</strong> — the dictionary proposition ("the door is
    closed"). Around it sit <strong>connotation</strong> (the emotional colouring: "slender"
    versus "skinny"), <strong>register</strong> (the formality level: contract language
    versus text messages), and <strong>pragmatics</strong> (what the utterance is doing:
    "can you pass the salt?" is a request, not a question about ability). Languages package
    these layers differently — Japanese encodes social hierarchy in verb endings; English
    does not — so identical denotations can carry different social meanings, and the
    translator must decide which layers matter for this text.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="the layers of meaning — file each example" widget="sort-buckets" config={trLayerSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>Equivalence</strong> is the field's word for "saying the same thing in another
    language" — and its central discovery is that equivalence comes in kinds. Eugene Nida's
    classic distinction still organises the craft. <strong>Formal equivalence</strong> keeps
    the source's structure visible: same sentence shapes, same imagery, even the same
    ambiguities — the choice for legal documents, scripture study and anything whose <em>form
    is its meaning</em>. <strong>Dynamic (or functional) equivalence</strong> reproduces the
    reader's <em>experience</em>: Nida's own example is rendering the biblical phrase "gird
    up your loins" for a modern audience as "prepare yourself for action", because the
    original's readers received an exhortation to readiness, not a clothing instruction.
    Neither pole is "the answer" — the text's function decides, and most real texts live
    between the poles, sentence by sentence.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="you set the equivalence — three texts, three positions" widget="branch" config={trEquivBranch} />
  <p>
    Later theory sharpened the idea rather than replacing it. Roman Jakobson's famous
    formulation — languages differ in what they <em>must</em> convey, not what they
    <em>may</em> convey — explains why full equivalence is impossible in principle (French
    must mark tu/vous; English must not) and yet translation always remains possible, because
    the message can be recoded. The skopos school (Vermeer) added the sharpest practical
    lens: translate for the <em>purpose</em> of the target text. A chocolate bar's German
    name becomes a French name chosen to sell chocolate in France, not to remain a German
    word — the brief, not the source, sets the goal.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a good translation is invisible and interchangeable with
    the original. Every translation is a reading — translators make interpretive choices
    visible or invisible, and two excellent translations of the same poem can differ
    substantially. Equivalence to the <em>effect</em> is the standard, not equivalence to
    the <em>words</em>.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — the invisible-translation myth" widget="judge" config={trInvisJudge} />

  <h2>Source-text analysis</h2>
  <p>
    Professional translation begins with reading the source for function — a deliberate
    analysis step amateurs skip and machines cannot perform. The classic questions:
    <strong>what is this text doing</strong> (informing, persuading, regulating,
    entertaining)? <strong>Who is it for</strong> (specialists, consumers, regulators, children)?
    <strong>What is its register and tone</strong> (legalese, breezy marketing, clinical
    neutrality)? <strong>What is its situation</strong> (where will the translation appear,
    and what must it accomplish there)? Professional practice often adds a formal
    <em>translation brief</em> — the commissioning client's statement of purpose, audience
    and deadline — which is precisely the skopos principle institutionalised.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the four questions of the analysis" widget="cloze" config={trAnalCloze} />
  <p>
    A worked contrast shows why analysis is not decoration. Take a liability clause:
    "The provider shall not be liable for any damages arising from use of the service."
    Here form is meaning — the legal effect lives in "shall not be liable" and "arising
    from" — so the translation must reproduce the same enforceable force in the target
    legal system's idiom, which may mean consulting the target jurisdiction's conventions
    rather than translating elegantly. Now take a slogan: "Have a break — have a KitKat."
    The meaning is not the words but the rhythm, the imperative warmth, the brand
    association — so the translation must <em>rebuild</em> those effects with new words,
    possibly a new joke, because the words themselves are not the product. Same language
    pair, completely different tasks — and only the analysis step tells you which task you
    are performing. Terminology work belongs here too: technical, legal and medical texts
    demand building the domain's term base <em>before</em> drafting, because in those
    registers a wrong term is a factual error.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="clause vs slogan — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={trTaskCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> knowing two languages fluently is enough to translate
  between them — that bilingualism and translation are the same skill. Bilingualism is the
  entry requirement, not the profession: the working skills are analysis (what the text is
  doing), writing in the target language at native register, terminology management, and
  the judgement about equivalence that neither fluency supplies. This is why the
  professional norm is translating <em>into</em> the native language, and why some superb
  bilinguals make poor translators.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> meaning arrives in layers — denotation, connotation, register,
    pragmatics — and equivalence is a choice between keeping the source's form and
    reproducing its effect, with skopos setting the goal. Next we meet the oldest argument in
    the field, and the spectrum that finally resolved it.
  </p>

  <h2>Literal versus free: the spectrum</h2>
  <p>
    The literal-versus-free debate is the field's oldest argument, and its history teaches
    the resolution. Cicero argued in the first century BCE that he translated speeches
    "not word for word, but sense for sense" (<em>non verbum e verbo, sed sensum exprimere
    de sensu</em>) — and Jerome, translating the Bible, took the same position, adding that
    scripture excepted, where the words themselves are mysteries. For most of two millennia
    the argument ran as a contest. The modern field resolves it as a <strong>spectrum</strong>
    with positions chosen per text and per sentence: word-for-word (gloss), literal,
    faithful, semantic, communicative, free, adaptation. The translator's skill is knowing
    which position a given sentence demands — and being able to defend the choice.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="the spectrum, from gloss to adaptation" widget="order" config={trSpecOrder} />
  <p>
    When is literal right? More often than beginners expect: when the reader needs the
    source's structure (a manual that a technician will cross-reference in the original),
    when the form carries legal or ritual weight, when the client wants a translation that
    tracks the source for audit. When is free necessary? Whenever the literal output would
    confuse, mislead or bore — which is the predictable fate of idioms, humour, poetry and
    advertising under literal translation. The professional habit is to default to the
    <em>freest version that preserves the function</em>, then tighten toward literal only
    where the text type requires it — the inverse of the amateur's habit, which starts
    literal and panics.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the most literal translation is the most faithful one —
  that closeness to the source's words is a measure of accuracy. Fidelity is to the text's
  <em>function</em>, and a literal rendering of an idiom, a joke or an advertisement can be
  perfectly accurate about the words while completely false about the message — the German
  reader told that pets are falling from the sky has been misinformed, not informed. The
  professional default is the freest version that preserves the function, tightened toward
  the literal only when the text type demands it.</p>

  <h2>Loss is the honest baseline</h2>
  <p>
    Every translation loses something: wordplay, metre, cultural resonance, register. The
    professional skill is deliberate <strong>compensation</strong> — reproducing a pun's
    <em>effect</em> with a different pun, preserving formality where the joke was, footnoting
    where neither works, adding rhythm where the allusion was dropped. The classic cases
    teach the method: translating "it's raining cats and dogs" into a language with its own
    rain idiom borrows the idiom (effect preserved); translating a novel's pun on a
    character's name invents a new name whose new pun carries the humour; translating
    haiku's seventeen syllables chooses which of the form's features — syllable count,
    seasonal image, restraint — to preserve, because keeping all three is not possible in
    most target languages. Unmanaged loss is the hallmark of machine-literal output; managed
    loss is the human value that post-editing still requires.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the literal-versus-free argument ends as a spectrum chosen per
    text and per sentence, with the professional default being the freest version that
    preserves the function. Loss, though, is unavoidable on every point of that spectrum — so
    next we look at how professionals manage it honestly.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example: three sentences, three strategies</h2>
  <p>
    Watching decisions happen sentence by sentence is the fastest way to internalise the
    theory. Take three source sentences an English-to-something translator might meet in one
    morning. First, a manual's "Press the red button to stop the machine." — here formal
    equivalence wins: short, functional, instruction-shaped; the translation matches the
    structure and resists embellishment, because a maintenance technician may be comparing
    it against the original. Second, a novel's "The city exhaled as the last train left." —
    the meaning is not the verb but the mood of collective relief; a literal "breathed out"
    would read as a lung report, so the translator builds the target language's own image of
    a city relaxing at night, preserving register and atmosphere over structure. Third, an
    idiom: "Let's play it by ear." — whose surface (playing, ears) is nonsense in almost
    every target language; the correct unit is the pragmatic meaning ("we'll improvise as we
    go"), so the translator reaches for the target language's own idiom or a plain natural
    equivalent. Three sentences, three positions on the formal–dynamic spectrum, all chosen
    by the text's function — which is the entire foundational unit, performed.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A word-for-word rendering of "it's raining cats and dogs" fails as translation. What exactly does it fail to carry across, and what would succeed?</li>
    <li>You must translate a liability clause for a contract and, an hour later, an advertising slogan. Why do the two texts demand opposite positions on the formal-dynamic spectrum?</li>
    <li>Is fluent bilingualism enough to make someone a good translator? Name two professional skills it does not supply.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It carries the words but loses the message — the target
    reader is told pets are falling from the sky, which misinforms; the meaning ("it is raining
    very hard") expressed the way a native speaker would is what succeeds. (2) In the clause,
    form is meaning: the enforceable force lives in the wording, so formal equivalence wins.
    In the slogan, the meaning is rhythm, warmth and brand association, which must be rebuilt
    with new words — dynamic equivalence wins. (3) No — bilingualism is the entry requirement,
    not the profession; it does not by itself supply source-text analysis (what the text is
    doing, for whom) or the equivalence judgement and target-language writing skill at native
    register, which is why professionals translate into their native language.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The unit of translation work is the message, not the word — a word-for-word rendering of an idiom is misinformation, not translation.</li>
    <li>Meaning has layers — denotation, connotation, register, pragmatics — and a translation fails at whichever layer it ignores.</li>
    <li>Formal equivalence keeps the source's structure visible; dynamic equivalence reproduces the reader's experience; the text's function decides.</li>
    <li>Professional translation begins with source-text analysis: what the text is doing, for whom, in what register, in what situation.</li>
    <li>The literal-versus-free debate resolves as a spectrum chosen per sentence, with the default being the freest version that preserves the function.</li>
    <li>Every translation loses something; the professional skill is deliberate compensation, and unmanaged loss is the hallmark of machine-literal output.</li>
    <li>The reader is the test: a translation succeeds when its audience receives the effect the source audience received.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With equivalence, analysis and managed loss in place, later lessons in this programme add
    the working layers: the shift techniques of Vinay and Darbelnet, CAT tools and term bases,
    the specialist domains, and the economics and ethics of professional practice. The move
    taught on this page — analyse, choose, manage the loss, test on the reader — is the
    foundation all of it rests on.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Equivalence has a formal taxonomy</strong> worth meeting early. Vinay and
    Darbelnet catalogued the actual <em>shifts</em> translators make — borrowing (the word
    crosses over: "le weekend"), calque (the structure crosses over: "skyscraper" becoming
    <em>gratte-ciel</em>), modulation (same meaning, different viewpoint: "it is not
    difficult" for "it is easy"), and transposition (grammar-class change: a noun in one
    language, a verb in the other). Reading a good translation with this list in hand turns
    it from magic into craft: every sentence is a decision, and the decisions have names.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The profession's frame</strong> makes the theory testable. The American
    Translators Association's certification exam scores against a published error typology —
    accuracy, terminology, grammar and style, weighted by severity — so "equivalence"
    becomes gradable: a mistranslation of meaning is a major deduction; a stiff sentence is
    a minor one. Ethics bind the trade: confidentiality, fidelity to the source, working
    only into the native language (the near-universal professional norm), and declining
    assignments outside one's competence. Quality standards for translation projects
    (ISO 17100 in Europe) formalise the same expectations — qualified translators,
    revision by a second professional, documented feedback.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Machine translation sharpens the human question rather than dissolving it.</strong>
    Modern neural systems produce fluent output and handle routine denotative text well —
    which has restructured the industry around post-editing and specialisation. But the
    systems' characteristic failures are exactly this unit's subject: they translate
    <em>words and patterns</em>, not purpose, so they miss register, function, humour,
    deliberate ambiguity and the translation brief itself. The enduring human value is the
    move this page teaches: analyse what the text is doing, choose the equivalence, manage
    the loss, and check the result against the reader. Later units add the CAT tools, the
    domains and the economics — but all of it rests on that move.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A closing distinction the field draws early: translation versus
    localisation.</strong> Translation carries a text across languages; localisation
    adapts a <em>product</em> — software, a website, a game — to a locale: its language,
    yes, but also its date and number formats, currency, images, colour associations,
    legal notices and idioms of interaction. The software term that must stay English in
    one market, the checkout flow reordered for a region's payment habits, the joke that
    needs a regional replacement — these are localisation decisions, made by teams and
    governed by style guides, glossaries and testing in the target market. The two
    disciplines share this unit's foundation completely (analysis, equivalence, the
    reader as test), and every large-scale translation project today has localisation
    dimensions. Knowing which one a task is — and which quality standard and toolchain it
    implies — is part of the professional's first analysis of any assignment.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ATA Certification Program content outline; Nida &amp; Taber, <em>The Theory and Practice of Translation</em>; Munday, <em>Introducing Translation Studies</em>. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
